  "slug": "untitled-topic-29c0",
  "summary": "Limited cross-source concordance of drug-induced responses and new challenge metrics push perturbation models toward explicit noise and structure handling.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that perturbation-response prediction depends on data reliability and evaluation design. A six-resource comparison reports high treated-expression agreement but limited cross-source concordance of drug-induced responses, while the Virtual Cell Challenge 2026 specifies six metrics for zero-shot CRISPRi prediction. Several methods build in noise or structure: DeepCINET models viability assay noise, siGMA applies heteroscedastic uncertainty to noisy cross-dataset siRNA labels, GFM combines knowledge graphs with latent flow matching for unseen Perturb-seq genes, GAIN learns non-additive interactions over an additive baseline, and CIRCE exploits combinatorial perturbation structure to infer graded signaling activity. PTM-BDL extends drug-response prediction to site-resolved phosphosite features. These are preprints, so the reported performance gains remain unreviewed.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
